# install/impacket-binaries.lst — every `impacket-*` command Kali puts on PATH.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. test/check-corpus-commands.sh is offline and REQUIRED, so it
# cannot ask apt whether an `impacket-something` is real. It also must not ask the local
# box: these binaries only exist where the packages are installed, and a maintainer's
# laptop would then decide which corpus lines pass. So the roster is checked in.
#
# This is the list that makes `impacket-petitpotam` FAIL. The `impacket-` prefix is not a
# licence — membership here is (dotgibson/dotfiles-Offense#208). PetitPotam is
# topotam/PetitPotam and was never an impacket script; it shipped in a corpus entry and no
# gate on either side could see it.
#
# ⚠ TWO PACKAGES, NOT ONE. The manifest lists `impacket-scripts` (57 binaries), but five
# more — including `impacket-secretsdump` and `impacket-wmiexec`, two of the most-used —
# come from `python3-impacket`, which impacket-scripts Depends on. A roster built from
# impacket-scripts alone would fail exactly those two. This file is the UNION.
#
# ⚠ CASE IS SIGNIFICANT. Kali names each wrapper after the example file, so it is
# `impacket-GetUserSPNs` and `impacket-getST`, not lowercase.
#
# REGENERATE (advisory step in .github/workflows/packages.yml, kali-rolling container):
#   for p in impacket-scripts python3-impacket; do
#     apt-get download "$p" && dpkg -c "$p"_*.deb | grep '/usr/bin/' |
#       sed 's|.*/usr/bin/||; s| ->.*||'
#   done | grep . | sort -u
# Derived from impacket-scripts 1.10 + python3-impacket on kali-rolling, 2026-08-21.
